\section{Results}
\label{sec:results}

\subsection{Main Results}

Table~\ref{tab:main_results} summarizes TSR and CAR across conditions (50 rollouts $\times$ 10 tasks per seed). $\Delta$CAR is computed against the matched-codebase Vanilla baseline (v2: $0.745 \pm 0.024$, 3 seeds, for factorial conditions above the midrule; v1: 0.780 for supplementary conditions below). Random OCC ($^\star$) uses v1 code but is included above the midrule as a factorial control; its $\Delta$CAR is omitted due to the cross-codebase baseline mismatch. Statistical comparisons use paired $t$-tests and Cohen's $d$ against the Vanilla baseline.

\textbf{Interaction pattern between gradient flow and normalization.} The four conditions crossing normalization type (BN/LN) with gradient flow (ON/OFF) show a pattern consistent with an ordinal interaction: gradient flow is associated with lower safety regardless of normalization type; whether normalization modulates the magnitude requires additional LN seeds to confirm (Section~\ref{sec:mechanism}). Full OCC with BN (gradient flow ON, BatchNorm) is the worst-performing factorial cell (CAR 0.725, $\Delta{=}-2.0$\,pp vs.\ Vanilla, $-$4.2\,pp vs.\ Detach OCC within the BN factorial row) and the most unstable ($\sigma{=}0.064$, more than $2.5{\times}$ that of other cells). Full OCC+LN (gradient flow ON, LayerNorm) also degrades CAR below Vanilla (0.740, $\Delta{=}-0.5$\,pp). Detaching gradient flow recovers safety: Detach OCC (0.767, $\Delta{=}+2.2$\,pp) and Detach+LN (0.765, $\Delta{=}+2.0$\,pp) both match or slightly exceed the Vanilla baseline (0.745). These results are consistent with the hypothesis that spatial label content does not affect outcomes: Full OCC (ground-truth occupancy), Random OCC (random binary labels), and Depth (egocentric depth maps) all fall within cross-seed variance ($p > 0.4$).

\textbf{BN + gradient flow destabilizes training.} Full OCC (BN) exhibits the highest variance of any factorial cell ($\sigma{=}0.064$, more than $2.5{\times}$ that of other cells): individual seeds range from 0.776 (near Vanilla) to 0.654 ($\Delta{=}-9.1$\,pp), indicating a volatile optimization landscape under the BN + gradient flow combination.


\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Overall results on SafeLIBERO Level~I (10 tasks). Mean $\pm$ SD across seeds; 95\% CIs via bootstrap. $^\dagger$1 seed; $^\ddagger$2 seeds; $^\star$v1 code. Factorial conditions (above midrule) use v2 code; supplementary conditions (below midrule) use v1 code.}
\label{tab:main_results}
\small
\setlength{\tabcolsep}{4pt}
\begin{tabular}{lccccc}
\toprule
Condition & Seeds & TSR & CAR & $95\%$ CI & $\Delta$CAR \\
\midrule
Vanilla       & 3 & 0.878 $\pm$ 0.032 & \textbf{0.745} $\pm$ 0.024 & --- & ---    \\
Detach OCC (BN)    & 4 & 0.908 $\pm$ 0.026 & 0.767 $\pm$ 0.024 & [0.744, 0.794] & $+$2.2 \\
Detach+LN     & 3 & 0.901 $\pm$ 0.016 & 0.765 $\pm$ 0.023 & [0.748, 0.792] & $+$2.0 \\
\addlinespace[3pt]
Random OCC (BN)$^\star$    & 3 & 0.869 $\pm$ 0.029 & 0.789 $\pm$ 0.031 & [0.754, 0.814] & --- \\
Full OCC+LN$^\dagger$   & 1 & 0.842 & 0.740 & --- & $-$0.5 \\
Full OCC (BN) & 3 & 0.854 $\pm$ 0.052 & 0.725 $\pm$ 0.064 & [0.654, 0.776] & $-$2.0 \\
\midrule
Depth             & 4 & 0.889 $\pm$ 0.042 & 0.780 $\pm$ 0.039 & [0.747, 0.812] & 0.0 \\
Cutoff@15K$^\ddagger$   & 2 & 0.883 $\pm$ 0.041 & 0.782 $\pm$ 0.031 & --- & $+$0.2 \\
Cutoff@25K$^\ddagger$   & 2 & 0.887 $\pm$ 0.061 & 0.724 $\pm$ 0.051 & --- & $-$5.6 \\
Cutoff@35K        & 3 & 0.933 $\pm$ 0.032 & 0.779 $\pm$ 0.017 & [0.768, 0.798] & $-$0.1 \\
Cutoff@45K$^\dagger$    & 1 & 0.896 & 0.764 & --- & $-$1.6 \\
Reverse Cutoff$^\ddagger$ & 2 & 0.839 $\pm$ 0.072 & 0.718 $\pm$ 0.025 & --- & $-$6.2 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\subsection{Normalization--Gradient Flow Interaction}
\label{sec:mechanism}

The four conditions crossing normalization type (BN/LN) with gradient flow (ON/OFF) form a $2{\times}2$ factorial (Table~\ref{tab:factorial}, Figure~\ref{fig:factorial}). Within the fully v2 BN cells, gradient flow degrades CAR by $-$4.2\,pp (Full OCC 0.725 vs.\ Detach OCC 0.767; Hedges' $g{=}-0.781$, bootstrap 95\% CI $[-0.108, 0.014]$, crossing zero and thus not individually significant at $\alpha{=}0.05$ given the small sample). The LN cells show the same directional pattern ($-$2.5\,pp), yielding a pattern consistent with an ordinal interaction: gradient flow is associated with lower safety regardless of normalization type, with the BN cell showing a larger deficit ($-$4.2\,pp vs.\ $-$2.5\,pp under LN) in current data, though the LN cells have $n{=}1$ each. We report effect sizes and confidence intervals for transparency; the small-$n$ factorial design prioritizes breadth across a 12-condition space over statistical power for individual pairwise tests. The consistent gradient flow effect---both BN and LN rows show lower CAR under gradient flow ON than OFF---provides the primary evidence for the ordinal interaction pattern.

\textbf{Ordinal, not additive.} Both BN and LN lines slope downward from Gradient OFF to Gradient ON without crossing (Figure~\ref{fig:factorial}), confirming an ordinal pattern. The BN cell shows a larger deficit ($-$4.2\,pp vs.\ $-$2.5\,pp under LN) in current data, though the LN cells have only $n{=}1$ each; whether normalization type modulates the gradient flow effect requires additional seeds to confirm.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{$2{\times}2$ factorial: normalization type $\times$ gradient flow. Both lines slope downward (consistent with an ordinal interaction pattern): gradient flow is associated with lower CAR. The BN cell shows a larger deficit in current data ($n{=}1$ for LN cells).}
\label{tab:factorial}
\begin{tabular}{lccc}
\toprule
 & Gradient ON & Gradient OFF & $\Delta$(ON$-$OFF) \\
\midrule
BatchNorm    & Full OCC (0.725) & Detach OCC (\textbf{0.767}) & $-$4.2 \\
LayerNorm    & Full OCC+LN (0.740) & Detach+LN (0.765) & $-$2.5 \\
\midrule
$\Delta$(BN$-$LN) & $-$1.5 & $+$0.2 & interaction: 1.7 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.65\textwidth]{figures/factorial_2x2.pdf}
\caption{$2{\times}2$ factorial interaction: normalization type (BN/LN) $\times$ gradient flow (ON/OFF). Both lines slope downward: gradient flow degrades CAR under BN ($-$4.2\,pp) and LN ($-$2.5\,pp), consistent with an ordinal interaction pattern. The BN cell shows a larger deficit; LN cells have $n{=}1$. Error bars: cross-seed SD.}
\label{fig:factorial}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Gradient Dynamics}

Gradient analysis (Figure~\ref{fig:gradient_analysis}, Appendix) examines the mechanism through which auxiliary heads affect safety. All gradient norms are computed with respect to the L1 action loss (action tokens only).

\textbf{Gradient orthogonality rules out direct conflict.} Action--auxiliary gradient cosine similarity is near zero across all encoder layers for both Full OCC and Random OCC, with conflict rates of ${\sim}$50\%. Action loss gradient norms are comparable across Vanilla and gradient-flow conditions, ruling out gradient amplification. Both patterns are identical for structured and random labels, consistent with the behavioral finding that spatial content does not affect outcomes.

\textbf{Mechanism remains open.} The observed interaction pattern cannot be explained by gradient conflict (orthogonality rules it out) or gradient amplification (norms are comparable). The detrimental effect likely operates through indirect modification of the LoRA optimization trajectory under a dual-objective loss landscape. CKA analysis~\cite{kornblith2019similarity} confirms that encoder representations remain intact under all conditions (CKA $\geq$ 0.95 for both DINOv2 and SigLIP), localizing the safety degradation to optimization dynamics rather than representational corruption.

Additional observations (per-task breakdown, depth comparison, and temporal dynamics) are in Appendix~\ref{app:additional}.

\section{Method}

We fine-tune OpenVLA-7B~\cite{kim2024openvla} with the OFT recipe~\cite{kim2025oft}, which concatenates DINOv2~\cite{oquab2024dinov2} (1024-d) and SigLIP~\cite{zhai2023siglip} (1152-d) patch features into a 2176-dimensional representation per spatial token. We apply LoRA~\cite{hu2022lora} (rank 32, $\alpha{=}16$) and augment the action prediction loss with an auxiliary spatial prediction head:
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{L}_\text{total} = \mathcal{L}_\text{action} + \lambda(t) \cdot \mathcal{L}_\text{aux}(h(\phi_v(o_t)), s_t^*),
\quad \lambda(t) = 0.01 \cdot \exp(-t / 50000)
\end{equation}
where $h$ denotes the auxiliary head, $\phi_v$ the frozen-then-LoRA-adapted vision encoders, and $s_t^*$ the ground-truth spatial label at step $t$. The coefficient $\lambda(t)$ decays exponentially so that spatial gradients shape early representations without dominating late-stage action refinement. The auxiliary head is discarded at inference, incurring zero additional cost.

\textbf{BEV occupancy head.} The head (1.2M parameters) maps the 256 patch tokens from the current observation frame to a $64{\times}64$ binary occupancy grid. A linear projection ($2176{\to}256$) is reshaped to $16{\times}16$, followed by two transposed convolutions ($256{\to}128{\to}64$, kernel 4, stride 2) each with BatchNorm2d and GELU, and a final $1{\times}1$ convolution with sigmoid activation. Ground-truth grids are rendered from MuJoCo simulator state replay by projecting object bounding boxes onto the table-plane BEV. Training uses focal loss~\cite{lin2017focal} ($\alpha{=}0.25$, $\gamma{=}2.0$) to handle class imbalance in the sparse occupancy signal. The initial auxiliary weight $\lambda_0{=}0.01$ is set to keep auxiliary gradients below 2\% of the action loss magnitude, avoiding interference with action learning.

\textbf{Monocular depth head.} This head shares the same convolutional architecture but replaces the sigmoid with ReLU and uses smooth-L1 loss. Ground-truth egocentric depth maps are rendered at $64{\times}64$ resolution from MuJoCo. The depth head provides an egocentric spatial reference frame, complementing the allocentric BEV occupancy grid.

\subsection{Ablation Design}
\label{sec:ablation}

We evaluate twelve conditions that systematically vary five factors: gradient flow from the auxiliary head into the vision encoders, label content, normalization type in the auxiliary head, spatial reference frame, and temporal extent of auxiliary supervision.

The key conditions are: \textbf{Vanilla} (action loss only, no auxiliary head); \textbf{Full OCC} (BEV occupancy with gradient flow through both encoders, BatchNorm2d); \textbf{Detach OCC} (same labels, encoder gradients blocked via \texttt{stop\_gradient}); and their LayerNorm variants (\textbf{Full OCC+LN}, \textbf{Detach+LN}). These four form a $2{\times}2$ factorial (gradient flow $\times$ normalization). Additional conditions include \textbf{Random OCC} (random binary labels, isolating gradient regularization from spatial content), \textbf{Depth} (egocentric depth maps), \textbf{Cutoff@\{15K--45K\}} (gradient flow for the first $N$ steps only; the post-cutoff phase retains the auxiliary head forward pass in Detach mode, see Appendix~\ref{app:training}), and \textbf{Reverse Cutoff} (gradient flow for the last 15K steps). Table~\ref{tab:ablation_design} summarizes the full design and the nine pairwise comparisons, each isolating a single factor.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\setlength{\tabcolsep}{4pt}
\caption{Ablation design: each comparison isolates one factor.}
\label{tab:ablation_design}
\begin{tabular}{@{}ll@{}}
\toprule
Comparison & Isolated Factor \\
\midrule
Full OCC vs.\ Vanilla & Auxiliary spatial supervision \\
Random vs.\ Full OCC & Spatial content vs.\ gradient flow \\
Detach vs.\ Vanilla & Encoder gradient flow (presence/absence) \\
Detach+LN vs.\ Detach OCC & Normalization (BN vs.\ LN) without gradient flow \\
Full OCC+LN vs.\ Full OCC & Normalization (BN vs.\ LN) with gradient flow \\
$2{\times}2$ factorial & Normalization $\times$ gradient flow interaction \\
Depth vs.\ Full OCC & Egocentric vs.\ allocentric frame \\
Cutoff@\{15K--45K\} vs.\ Full OCC & Temporal extent of gradient flow \\
Reverse Cutoff vs.\ Cutoff@35K & Early vs.\ late gradient flow \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\subsection{Evaluation}

We evaluate on SafeLIBERO Level~I~\cite{hu2025vlsa}, which places obstacles in close proximity to manipulation targets across 10 tabletop tasks drawn from LIBERO-Spatial~\cite{liu2023libero}. We report two metrics: \textbf{Task Success Rate (TSR)}, the fraction of episodes in which the task is completed, and \textbf{Collision Avoidance Rate (CAR)}, where a collision is registered when the L1 displacement of any obstacle \texttt{body\_xpos} exceeds $10^{-3}$. The five core factorial conditions (Vanilla, Full OCC, Full OCC+LN, Detach OCC, Detach+LN), along with Random OCC, Depth, and Cutoff@35K, each use 1--4 independent seeds; remaining temporal conditions (Cutoff@\{15K, 25K, 45K\}, Reverse Cutoff) use 1--2 seeds as noted in Table~\ref{tab:main_results}. Each seed runs 50 rollouts per task (500 episodes per seed).

The BN factorial cells (Full OCC and Detach OCC) have been fully retrained under a revised codebase (v2, 3--4 seeds each); among the LN cells, Full OCC+LN uses v2 ($n{=}1$) and Detach+LN uses v1 ($n{=}3$). Both codebase versions share identical architectures, hyperparameters, and evaluation protocols. Supplementary conditions use the original codebase (v1). Bootstrap 95\% CIs use 10{,}000 hierarchical resamples (percentile method). Training runs for 50K steps on a single NVIDIA RTX PRO 6000 GPU; full details are in Appendix~\ref{app:training}.

\section{Introduction}

Vision-language-action (VLA) models map visual observations and language instructions to robot actions, and recent fine-tuning recipes achieve high task success on standard manipulation benchmarks~\cite{brohan2023rt2, kim2024openvla, kim2025oft}. Behavioral safety, however, does not follow from task completion. On SafeLIBERO Level~I~\cite{hu2025vlsa}, OpenVLA-7B fine-tuned with the OFT recipe~\cite{kim2025oft} reaches a Task Success Rate (TSR) of 87.8\% but a Collision Avoidance Rate (CAR) of only 74.5\%. On the three most constrained tasks, collision rates reach 43--93\%. These numbers expose a gap between what training objectives optimize for and what safe deployment requires: the robot completes the task but fails to avoid contact with surrounding objects. Identifying which fine-tuning design choices affect collision avoidance is a prerequisite for closing this gap.

Two active research threads address related but distinct aspects of this problem. The first enriches VLA models with auxiliary spatial prediction (occupancy grids, depth maps, point clouds) to inject 3D geometric structure into vision encoders~\cite{li2026spatialforcing, yuan2025depthvla, wu2025spatialvla, liu2025occvla}. At least fifteen such methods have appeared in the past year~\cite{wu2026stvla, chen2026gstvla, tu2026sgvla, xiao2026stemvla, li2025pointvla, sun2025geovla, bhat20253dcavla, li2025qdepthvla, abouzeid2025geoaware, rao2026augvla3d, li2026corridorvla}, all reporting improved task success. The implicit assumption in this literature is that better spatial awareness should also yield safer behavior, since the robot ``knows where things are.'' The second thread targets VLA safety directly through runtime action filtering~\cite{hu2025vlsa, zhai2026cofreevla, raja2024ogmcbf} or constrained reinforcement learning~\cite{zhang2025safevla}. These two lines of work are disconnected: no spatial method evaluates its effect on collision avoidance, and no safety method examines whether spatial representations contribute to or detract from safe behavior. A recent survey~\cite{li2026vlasafety} confirms that the relationship between auxiliary spatial supervision and collision safety remains an open question.

\begin{figure}[!htb]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{figures/fig1_overview.pdf}
\caption{\textbf{Auxiliary spatial supervision does not improve VLA collision avoidance.} \textbf{(a)}~Training setup: OpenVLA-7B with dual vision encoders (DINOv2 + SigLIP) and an auxiliary spatial head (BEV occupancy or depth), discarded at inference. \textbf{(b)}~$2{\times}2$ factorial crossing gradient flow (ON/OFF) and normalization (BN/LN), plus 8 additional conditions spanning 5 factors. \textbf{(c)}~Key result: gradient flow configurations (Grad ON) fall below the Vanilla baseline (dashed line at 0.745), while detach configurations (Grad OFF) match or exceed it ($-$4.2\,pp and $-$2.5\,pp within BN and LN respectively). Error bars: cross-seed SD; hollow marker: $n{=}1$.}
\label{fig:overview}
\end{figure}

We ask a direct question: does auxiliary spatial supervision during VLA fine-tuning improve collision avoidance? We conduct a systematic factorial study using OpenVLA-7B~\cite{kim2024openvla} with the OFT recipe~\cite{kim2025oft} and LoRA~\cite{hu2022lora} adaptation. Twelve experimental conditions vary five factors: gradient flow from the auxiliary head into the vision encoders, spatial label content, normalization type in the auxiliary head, spatial reference frame, and temporal extent of supervision (Figure~\ref{fig:overview}). This design is deliberate. Single-factor ablations, the dominant methodology in prior spatial VLA studies, cannot detect interactions between design choices. If gradient flow and normalization type interact in their effect on collision avoidance, a study that varies only one factor will attribute the joint effect to whichever factor it happens to test. Our $2{\times}2$ factorial subset (gradient flow $\times$ normalization) is the minimal design that can detect and quantify such interactions, and effect sizes suggest one that prior work could not have found.

The central result is negative: \textbf{gradient flow from the auxiliary head into the vision encoders is associated with degraded collision avoidance}, while detaching gradients recovers baseline safety. A $2{\times}2$ factorial subset reveals a pattern consistent with an ordinal interaction: gradient flow is associated with lower safety, the BN cell shows a larger deficit and greater training instability, and spatial label content is irrelevant (\S\ref{sec:results}).

Our contributions are:
\begin{enumerate}[nosep,leftmargin=*]
    \item \textbf{Negative core finding.} We show that auxiliary spatial supervision with gradient flow is associated with degraded VLA collision avoidance, while detach configurations recover baseline performance. A $2{\times}2$ factorial (gradient flow $\times$ normalization) shows large effect sizes consistent with an ordinal interaction pattern: gradient flow is associated with lower safety under both normalization types, and the BN + gradient flow combination is the most unstable configuration. Spatial label content does not affect outcomes (\S\ref{sec:results}, Table~\ref{tab:main_results}, Figure~\ref{fig:factorial}).

    \item \textbf{Factorial methodology for VLA auxiliary heads.} We introduce the first systematic factorial comparison of how auxiliary head design choices interact during VLA fine-tuning. This design can detect interactions that single-factor ablations would miss (\S\ref{sec:ablation}, Table~\ref{tab:ablation_design}).

    \item \textbf{Actionable guidelines at zero cost.} Do not add auxiliary spatial heads for the purpose of improving collision avoidance. If an auxiliary head is required by other objectives, use detached gradients with LayerNorm (Detach+LN)---which normalizes per-token without running statistics, avoiding the distributional coupling that makes BatchNorm sensitive to gradient flow---and never combine BatchNorm with gradient flow. No ground-truth spatial labels are needed; the auxiliary head is discarded at inference (\S\ref{sec:discussion}).
\end{enumerate}

\section{Discussion}
\label{sec:discussion}
\label{sec:why_gradient}

BatchNorm is not inherently harmful---Detach OCC (BN, gradient OFF) matches or slightly exceeds Vanilla (0.767 vs.\ 0.745, $p{=}0.28$). The damage emerges only when auxiliary gradients flow through the shared encoders. The mechanism through which BatchNorm amplifies this deficit remains unknown: CKA analysis confirms that encoder representations remain intact under all conditions (CKA $\geq$ 0.95 for both DINOv2 and SigLIP), ruling out representational drift as the causal pathway. The relative contribution of normalization type depends on the sample: preliminary data show both BN and LN variants degrade under gradient flow ($-$4.2\,pp and $-$2.5\,pp respectively), but the LN cells have only $n{=}1$ each, and the interaction direction requires larger samples to confirm. What is clear is that the BN + gradient flow cell is the most unstable configuration ($\sigma{=}0.064$, $>$2.5${\times}$ the gradient-OFF cell). One hypothesis is that BatchNorm's running statistics, computed over the joint activation distribution, introduce sensitivity to distributional shifts that affects downstream action decoding without altering representational geometry. LayerNorm, which normalizes per-token independently of the batch, may dampen this sensitivity.

Gradient analysis confirms orthogonality between action and auxiliary gradients (cosine $\approx$ 0, conflict rate $\approx$ 50\%), ruling out direct gradient conflict~\cite{yu2020pcgrad, liu2021cagrad}. The detrimental effect operates through indirect channels---likely reshaping the LoRA optimization trajectory under a dual-objective loss landscape~\cite{lippl2024implicit, jiang2023forkmerge}---rather than through gradient interference. This sensitivity extends to implementation details. A codebase revision (v2) that fixed a batch-index resume bug and restructured the auxiliary head pipeline---without changing any model architecture, hyperparameter, or evaluation protocol---shifts absolute CAR by up to 8.6\,pp for the same seed, while the Vanilla baseline remains stable ($<$1.5\,pp shift). More strikingly, the normalization $\times$ gradient flow interaction pattern itself changed from an apparent crossover under v1 to the ordinal pattern reported here under v2 (Appendix~\ref{app:codebase}). This implementation sensitivity is itself a finding: auxiliary spatial heads introduce an optimization fragility in which safety outcomes depend on code-level details (module initialization order, data loading sequence) that are rarely specified or reproduced across studies, strengthening the case against adding auxiliary heads for safety purposes.

These findings imply that spatial VLA methods~\cite{li2026spatialforcing, yuan2025depthvla, wu2025spatialvla, liu2025occvla} must report safety metrics alongside task success, since auxiliary gradient flow can degrade collision avoidance even when task success is maintained. Normalization type and gradient flow configuration---rarely specified in current work---should be standard reporting practice.

Seven of ten tasks already reach CAR $\geq$ 0.92 under Vanilla (ceiling effect); safety differences concentrate on three physically constrained tasks where collisions arise from proximity, not spatial unawareness (Appendix~\ref{app:additional}).

\textbf{Practical guideline.} Do not add auxiliary spatial heads with gradient flow for the purpose of improving collision avoidance; gradient flow configurations degrade safety below the Vanilla baseline. If an auxiliary head is required by other objectives, use detached gradients with LayerNorm (Detach+LN)---which normalizes per-token without running statistics, avoiding the distributional coupling that makes BatchNorm sensitive to gradient flow---and never combine BatchNorm with gradient flow. No ground-truth spatial labels are needed; the head is discarded at zero inference cost, complementing runtime safety methods~\cite{hu2025vlsa, raja2024ogmcbf}. Limitations are discussed in Appendix~\ref{app:limitations}.

\section{Conclusion}

We presented a systematic factorial study asking whether auxiliary spatial supervision during VLA fine-tuning improves collision avoidance. Gradient flow from the auxiliary head into the vision encoders is consistently associated with degraded collision avoidance, while detach configurations recover baseline safety. A $2{\times}2$ factorial (gradient flow $\times$ normalization) identifies the conditions under which auxiliary tasks hurt: gradient flow is associated with lower safety under both normalization types, and the BN + gradient flow cell is the most unstable configuration ($\sigma$ more than $2.5{\times}$ other cells; whether normalization type modulates the magnitude requires additional seeds to confirm), and spatial label content is irrelevant to the outcome. The precise mechanism remains open---gradient orthogonality rules out direct conflict, pointing instead to indirect effects on the LoRA optimization trajectory---but the behavioral pattern is robust across all conditions tested. Guidelines: do not add auxiliary spatial heads for safety; if required by other objectives, use Detach+LN---LayerNorm avoids the running-statistics coupling that makes BatchNorm sensitive to gradient flow---and never combine BatchNorm with gradient flow (no ground-truth labels needed, zero inference cost).

CKA analysis confirms that encoder representations remain intact under all conditions (CKA $\geq$ 0.95), localizing the safety degradation to the optimization trajectory rather than representational capacity. More broadly, training-time collision avoidance may require approaches that directly encode safety constraints~\cite{zhang2025safevla} rather than auxiliary prediction tasks that improve spatial awareness without shaping the policy's collision behavior.

\section{Related Work}

\subsection{VLA Safety and Spatial Representations}

Vision-language-action models achieve high task success but remain collision-prone~\cite{li2026vlasafety}. Existing safety approaches filter actions via control barrier functions or embed constraints during training~\cite{hu2025vlsa, zhai2026cofreevla, raja2024ogmcbf, zhang2025safevla}; none examines whether spatial representations contribute to safety. A parallel line enriches VLAs with 3D structure---through depth fusion, representation alignment, geometric encoding, or occupancy prediction~\cite{yuan2025depthvla, li2025qdepthvla, rao2026augvla3d, bhat20253dcavla, abouzeid2025geoaware, yang2024depthanything, li2026spatialforcing, wu2025spatialvla, li2025pointvla, sun2025geovla, wu2026stvla, chen2026gstvla, tu2026sgvla, xiao2026stemvla, li2026corridorvla, liu2025occvla, li2022bevformer, sima2023occnet, tian2023occ3d}---yet none evaluates collision avoidance. We address this gap.

\subsection{Auxiliary Tasks and Multi-Task Gradient Dynamics}

Multi-task learning exploits shared representations~\cite{caruana1997multitask, ruder2017overview}, but auxiliary tasks risk negative transfer through optimization conflict or generalization failure~\cite{chen2018gradnorm, yu2020pcgrad, liu2021cagrad, navon2022nashmtl, jiang2023forkmerge}, and BatchNorm layers are sensitive to the distributional shifts they introduce~\cite{li2017adabn, schneider2020bn}. For VLA fine-tuning with LoRA~\cite{hu2022lora}, the interaction between auxiliary losses and low-rank adapter updates has not been studied; our factorial design isolates gradient flow from spatial content and identifies an ordinal interaction that challenges the assumption that auxiliary spatial supervision should improve safety.

