\section{Additional Observations}
\label{app:additional}

\textbf{Per-task concentration.} Seven of ten tasks achieve CAR $\geq$ 0.92 under Vanilla; overall CAR is dominated by three physically constrained tasks (T3, T5, T6; Section~\ref{app:per_task}). Detach shows the lowest CAR on these hard tasks in v1 data (T3: 0.11, T5: 0.05). This v1 pattern does not replicate under the controlled v2 factorial design (Table~\ref{tab:main_results}), where detaching gradient flow consistently preserves safety (Detach OCC CAR 0.767 vs.\ Vanilla 0.745); the v1 per-task pattern may reflect implementation-specific artifacts rather than a generalizable effect of gradient detachment.

\textbf{Egocentric depth matches Vanilla (v1 data).} Depth (4 seeds) achieves CAR 0.780 $\pm$ 0.039, identical to Vanilla (v1, 0.780), despite providing per-pixel geometric information. Three label types (structured BEV, random binary, egocentric depth) yield comparable CAR when gradient flow is maintained.

\textbf{Temporal dynamics (preliminary, v1 data).} Cutoff@35K (3 seeds) achieves CAR 0.779, statistically indistinguishable from Vanilla (v1, $\Delta = -$0.1\,pp), which confirms that 35K steps of gradient flow fully protects collision avoidance. Surprisingly, Cutoff@25K (0.724, 2 seeds) and Reverse Cutoff (0.718, 2 seeds) fall substantially \emph{below} Vanilla. The most parsimonious explanation: setting the loss weight to zero retains the auxiliary head forward pass, so the post-cutoff period operates in Detach mode; BatchNorm interference acts on representations shaped by gradient flow, without gradient compensation. This is not withdrawal from gradient flow \emph{per se}, but transition into an actively harmful regime. Third seeds are in progress; extended analysis in Section~\ref{app:temporal}.

\section{Codebase Sensitivity (v1 vs.\ v2)}
\label{app:codebase}

The five core factorial conditions were retrained under a revised codebase (v2) that fixed a batch-index resume bug affecting cutoff timing and restructured the auxiliary head pipeline (module initialization order, data loading). All model architectures, hyperparameters, LoRA configuration, and evaluation protocols are identical between v1 and v2. The Vanilla baseline (no auxiliary head) is unaffected by these changes, since the revision touches only auxiliary-head code paths.

\textbf{Behavioral shift.} Under v1, Full OCC achieved a mean CAR of 0.797 (3 seeds, per-task Table~\ref{tab:per_task}); under v2, the same condition yields 0.725 $\pm$ 0.064 (Table~\ref{tab:main_results}). Same-seed comparisons show shifts of up to 8.6\,pp, while Vanilla remains stable across codebases ($<$1.5\,pp). This sensitivity is confined to conditions with auxiliary heads and is itself informative: it indicates that auxiliary head training dynamics are fragile with respect to implementation details that would not normally be considered hyperparameters.

\textbf{Gradient analysis shift.} The v1 codebase exhibited a Detach gradient suppression artifact: Detach action-loss gradient norms were only $\sim$4\% of Vanilla norms across all encoder layers. In v2, this suppression disappears entirely (Detach/Vanilla ratio $\approx$ 1.29), indicating that the v1 pattern was a code-path artifact rather than a genuine effect of gradient detachment. The gradient conflict rate also shifted from $\sim$44\% (v1) to $\sim$50\% (v2), with v2 values closer to the theoretical expectation for orthogonal vectors.

\textbf{Interaction pattern shift.} Under v1, the normalization $\times$ gradient flow interaction appeared as a crossover pattern ($p{=}0.025$); under v2, the pattern is ordinal (both lines slope downward without crossing). The crossover pattern in v1 is likely attributable to the Detach gradient suppression artifact described above, which differentially affected BN and LN conditions. All main-text analyses use v2 data exclusively; v1 results are retained in the appendix for completeness and to document the sensitivity.

\textbf{Implication.} The codebase sensitivity reinforces our central finding: auxiliary spatial heads introduce optimization fragility that implementation details can amplify or attenuate. This fragility is a further argument against adding auxiliary spatial heads for safety purposes, since the safety outcome depends on implementation choices that are difficult to specify, reproduce, or audit.

\section{Statistical Power Analysis}
\label{app:stat_power}

The $2{\times}2$ factorial design prioritizes breadth (12 conditions, 5 factors) over per-cell statistical power. For the BN row, the observed effect size is large (Hedges' $g{=}-0.781$), but with $n{=}3$ vs.\ $n{=}4$ seeds per cell, post-hoc power is approximately 0.3--0.4~\cite{cohen1988statistical}, meaning there is a 60--70\% probability of failing to detect a true effect of this magnitude at $\alpha{=}0.05$. We report this limitation transparently.

However, the study's evidential strength does not rest on any single pairwise comparison. The consistent gradient flow effect---both BN and LN rows show lower CAR under gradient flow ON than OFF---provides the primary evidence for the ordinal interaction pattern, complemented by the large observed effect size (Hedges' $g{=}-0.781$) in the fully powered BN row. Seed expansion ($n{=}5$ per factorial cell) is underway to increase per-cell power.

\section{Limitations}
\label{app:limitations}

Our study uses a single architecture (OpenVLA-OFT with LoRA rank 32); full fine-tuning may alter the interaction between auxiliary heads and encoder optimization because all parameters update simultaneously, potentially changing the gradient flow dynamics we observe. All experiments are conducted in MuJoCo simulation (SafeLIBERO Level~I, 10 tasks); real-world deployment introduces additional sources of distributional shift that could interact differently with auxiliary head training dynamics. The benchmark contains only static obstacles; dynamic obstacle scenarios may induce time-varying distributions where auxiliary head dynamics behave differently. Our collision metric is binary (L1 displacement threshold $10^{-3}$), which does not capture collision severity or near-miss behavior; a continuous safety metric might reveal finer-grained differences between conditions. CKA measures linear representational similarity, which may miss nonlinear structure relevant to safety behavior. The interaction pattern is based on $n{=}1$--$4$ seeds per cell; larger-scale replication would further strengthen conclusions. Cutoff conditions have 1--3 seeds, limiting temporal conclusions to preliminary observations.

